[s0l0!-header][h1]Operator Precedence[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

C evaluates a numeric expression based on [b]operator precedence[/b]. 
The + and – are equal in precedence, as are *, /, and %. 
The *, /, and % are performed first in order from left to right and then + and -, also in order from left to right.
You can change the order of operations by using parentheses ( ) to indicate which operations are to be performed first. 
For example, the result of 5 + 3 * 2 is 11, where the result of (5 + 3) * 2 is 16.

[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1628"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int a = 6;
  int b = 4;
  int c = 2;
  int result;
  result = a - b + c; // 4
  printf("%d \n", result);
 
  result = a + b / c; // 8
  printf("%d \n", result);
  
  result = (a + b) / c; // 5
  printf("%d \n", result);

  return 0;
}
[/code]
[note]C may not evaluate a numeric expression as desired when the associative property allows any order. For example, x*y*z may be evaluated as (x * y) * z or as x * (y * z). If order is important, break the expression into separate statements.[/note]

