[s0l0!-header][h1]Type Conversion[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

When a numeric expression contains operands of different data types, they are automatically converted as necessary in a process called [b]type conversion[/b].
For example, in an operation involving both floats and ints, the compiler will convert the int values to float values.
In the following program, the [b]increase [/b]variable is automatically converted to a [b]float[/b]: [code format="c" codeId="1629"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  float price = 6.50;
  int increase = 2;
  float new_price;

  new_price = price + increase;
  printf("New price is [b]%4.2f[/b]", new_price);
  /* Output: New price is 8.50 */

  return 0;
}[/code]
Note the format specifier includes 4.2 to indicate the float is to be printed in a space at least 4 characters wide with 2 decimal places.
When you want to force the result of an expression to a different type you can perform explicit type conversion by [b]type casting[/b], as in the statements: [code format="c" codeId="1636"]float average;
int total = 23;
int count = 4;

average = [b](float) [/b]total / count;
/* average = 5.75 */[/code]
Without the type casting, average will be assigned 5.
Explicit type conversion, even when the compiler may do automatic type conversion, is considered good programming style.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

